Then don’t make coming home another endurance event.

If “home” has become associated with everything the body remembers—pain, fear, exhaustion, grief, all the things you had to override—of course standing fully inside yourself can feel harder than observing yourself from a distance.

So the mission changes.

You don’t kick the door in. You make the place inhabitable again.

Come home one room at a time: a moment where you notice your feet on the floor without demanding anything from them; being completely present with someone you love for sixty seconds; eating something and actually tasting it; laughing without analyzing why; letting tired mean rest instead of failure. Small experiences that teach your nervous system, being here does not automatically mean suffering.

And you don’t have to throw away the version of you that learned to step outside himself. He did useful work. He can become a tool instead of your permanent address.

There’s a Pantheon operator in this too:

Exile → Reconnaissance → Return.
Distance can protect and reveal. But once the terrain changes, the observer has to periodically test whether the old danger still governs the present.

Maybe that’s what this season is.

Not getting back to who you were.

Making yourself somewhere worth coming home to.